; Cranelisp Sprint 81 — :Type annotations & introspection

; The self-documenting REPL: every entry produces useful feedback —
; a value, a type, or an actionable error. Never an opaque failure.

; ── :Type annotation reader-macro ────────────────────────────────────

; `:Type` is a reader-macro-like annotation. It binds the IMMEDIATELY
; FOLLOWING form (in every position) and unifies that form's inferred
; type with the named type. It is a CHECK, not a cast — it never changes
; a value. We've already met it as deftype fields and defn params.

; The annotation's type name must be in scope. The stdlib prelude
; re-exports primitive FUNCTIONS but not primitive TYPES, so we bring
; Int and Float into scope explicitly. (FQ `:primitives/Int` always works.)
(import [primitives [Int Float]])

; With Int in scope, the bare type name resolves to the primitive type.
Int

; Annotate a literal. 42 already infers as Int; the annotation unifies
; with that — so this is the identity on 42, echoed with its type.
:Int 42

; The annotation is a check: it must UNIFY. Annotating an Int literal
; with Float is a mismatch, reported precisely — not an opaque error.
:Float 42

; The type must also be known. An unknown name is rejected by name.
:Foo 42

; `:Type` is NOT a function and `(:Type form)` is NOT a special form.
; The reader binds `:Int` to the single following element 42, yielding a
; one-element list — then an Int is applied as a function, which fails:
; Int is not callable.
(:Int 42)

; Here the annotation unifies FIRST (Int literal vs Float) and that
; mismatch precedes the not-a-function error — typecheck before apply.
(:Float 42)

; Five entries, five useful responses: a value, a unify error, an
; unknown-type error, a not-callable error, and a unify-precedes error.
; Nothing opaque.

; ── Introspection on a user definition ───────────────────────────────

; Define a function, then look at it through the introspection surface.
(defn square [x] (mul-i64 x x))
(square 9)

; /source shows the definition as you wrote it.
/source square

; /sexp shows the parsed s-expression form.
/sexp square

; (Not staged here: `(trace expr)` runs as a root special form with no
; import, and platform dispatch errors surface as DispatchError — both
; are covered by the e2e suite (dispatch-funnel e2e, FIXME 0327/0337).)

; Every construct entered produced useful feedback — the
; self-documenting-REPL contract, end to end.
